StFstDbMaker/macro -- FST geometry (survey/alignment) tables
============================================================

What is here
------------
  fst/*.C                 the Geometry/fst survey tables, one file per
                          (table, beginTime) pair, in St_db_Maker's
                          <table>.<yyyymmdd>.<hhmmss>.C naming
  fstGeometry_db.C        reads those files and pushes them to the offline
                          MySQL DB (see "Pushing to the database" below)

These four tables are chained by StFstDb::loadGeometry() to place every FST
sensor:

      fstOnTpc            FST global position inside the TPC frame   (1 row)
      hssOnFst            each of the 2 half-support structures      (2 rows)
      fstWedgeOnHss       each wedge on its HSS                      (36 rows)
      fstSensorOnWedge    each sensor on its wedge                   (108 rows)

The DB is the truth. These files are the record of what was pushed, and the
way to override it locally.


Using these tables locally instead of the database
--------------------------------------------------
If you want to MODIFY the geometry parameters -- try a new alignment, test a
shift, reproduce an old iteration -- do NOT edit the database. St_db_Maker
looks for tables in this order:

      ./StarDb                <- your working directory, wins
      $STAR/StarDb            <- the release
      MySQL                   <- the database

so a copy in ./StarDb shadows everything else. Put it there:

      mkdir -p ./StarDb/Geometry
      cp -r $STAR/StRoot/StFstDbMaker/macro/fst ./StarDb/Geometry/fst
      # or: ln -s $STAR/StRoot/StFstDbMaker/macro/fst ./StarDb/Geometry/fst

Then edit the numbers in the copy, or add a new file with a later timestamp.

  * REMEMBER TO REMOVE IT when you want the database values back. A forgotten
    ./StarDb/Geometry/fst silently keeps you on your own numbers forever, and
    nothing in the log says so loudly.

  * CONDOR JOBS: the job runs in its own scratch directory, not where you
    submitted from, so ./StarDb must exist THERE. Copy or link the fst dir
    into the job's working/tmp directory as well, or the job silently falls
    back to the DB and your test does nothing. This is the single most common
    way an alignment test comes back "no change".

  * THE GEOMETRY CACHE MUST MATCH. StFwdTrackMaker does not read these tables
    at tracking time; it reads the cached fGeom.*.root built from them. If you
    change the tables you must rebuild the cache and point at the new one:

        root4star -b -q 'build_geom_misalign.C("dev2022sm","20211112.000003",1)'
            -> fGeom.dev2022sm.misalign1.sdt20211112.000003.root

    The sdt argument selects which table version AgPosition reads (the latest
    entry at or before that timestamp), and misalign=1 is what makes it read
    the survey tables at all. Copy or link the resulting fGeom file next to
    the job as well -- same scratch-directory trap as above.


What each timestamp means
-------------------------
All of these are Run 22 (FST commissioning). Translations are cm; no rotation
has ever been non-identity. Only fstOnTpc and hssOnFst are ever non-identity --
fstWedgeOnHss and fstSensorOnWedge are identity in every version to date.

IMPORTANT: none of these is an alignment iteration. Every non-identity number
here is THE SURVEY. $STAR/StarDb/Geometry/fst has exactly one timestamp,
20211110.000001, in which hssOnFst carries

      Left  half  t = (+0.07126, -0.30102, 0) cm
      Right half  t = (+0.22626, -0.33302, 0) cm

i.e. the survey says the FST halves sit 3.0-3.3 mm low in y and 0.7/2.3 mm off
in x -- the same order as the DB beamline y0 = -2.2 mm. Our files are three
treatments of that one survey, not successive measurements:

  file timestamp     content                                      meaning
  ---------------    ------------------------------------------   -----------------
  20211110.000001    all four tables identity, t = (0,0,0)        MISALIGNMENT OFF.
                                                                  A local override, NOT
                                                                  the release content
                                                                  at this name -- see
                                                                  the warning below
  20211111.000001    hssOnFst carries the survey as the release   THE SURVEY, verbatim
                     ships it; everything else identity:           (byte-identical to
                       Left  (+0.07126, -0.30102)                  $STAR's 20211110)
                       Right (+0.22626, -0.33302)
  20211112.000001    THE SAME SURVEY, re-decomposed: the common   The survey, split into
                     part lifted into fstOnTpc                     a common shift plus a
                       fstOnTpc (+0.14876, -0.31702)               differential
                     leaving a symmetric residual in hssOnFst
                       Left  (-0.07750, +0.01600)
                       Right (+0.07750, -0.01600)

  The last two are NOT successive iterations and carry no new information.
  fstOnTpc + hssOnFst sums to exactly the same total for both halves in both
  versions (verified element by element). 20211112 exists because the common
  offset belongs in fstOnTpc physically -- the whole detector sits low, plus a
  small differential between the halves -- and because an FST-global shift is
  then one number to iterate instead of two.

  So FST alignment has never been iterated past the survey. If and when it is,
  add a new timestamp; do not edit these.

  * WARNING, SAME FILENAME, DIFFERENT CONTENT. Our 20211110.000001 files have
    the same names as the release's, but ours are identity and the release's
    hssOnFst is the survey. Copying or linking this directory into ./StarDb
    therefore does not merely ADD entries -- it REPLACES the release survey
    with identity under the same name. Any run resolving to 20211110 then gets
    ideal geometry where $STAR would have given it the survey, silently. This
    is the sharpest form of the shadowing problem described above, and the
    reason to check for ./StarDb/Geometry/fst before trusting a result.

  The "comment" field in these files reads "Identity Matrix" in all of them,
  including the non-identity ones. It is stale text, and in any case
  memcpy(&row.comment,"...",1) copies a single byte, so the field carries no
  usable information. Do not rely on it; this README is the record.


