What each timestamp means
-------------------------
All of these are Run 22 (FST commissioning). The numbers are translations in
cm; no rotations have ever been non-identity in these tables.

  file timestamp     content                                      meaning
  ---------------    ------------------------------------------   -----------------
  20211110.000001    all four tables identity, t = (0,0,0)        initial: no shift,
                                                                  no rotation (ideal)
  20211111.000001    fstOnTpc identity; hssOnFst carries the      iteration 1 by Akio
                     full shift per HSS:
                       HSS0 (+0.07126, -0.30102)
                       HSS1 (+0.22626, -0.33302)
  20211112.000001    SAME ALIGNMENT AS 20211111, re-decomposed:   iteration 1 by Akio,
                     the common part lifted into fstOnTpc          re-decomposed
                       fstOnTpc (+0.14876, -0.31702)              (numerically
                     leaving a symmetric residual in hssOnFst      identical to
                       HSS0 (-0.07750, +0.01600)                   20211111)
                       HSS1 (+0.07750, -0.01600)

  Note on the last two: they are NOT successive iterations. fstOnTpc +
  hssOnFst sums to exactly the same total for both HSSs in both versions
  (verified element by element). 20211112 exists because putting the common
  offset in fstOnTpc is the physically meaningful split -- the whole detector
  moved, plus a small differential between the two halves -- and because an
  FST-global shift is then one number to iterate instead of two.

  No survey-derived FST table has ever been entered; every non-identity entry
  here comes from track-residual alignment on real data.

  The "comment" field in these files reads "Identity Matrix" in all of them,
  including the non-identity ones. It is stale text, and in any case
  memcpy(&row.comment,"...",1) copies a single byte, so the field carries no
  usable information. Do not rely on it; this README is the record.



The "misalign" BFC option is what makes any of this take effect
---------------------------------------------------------------
The AGML/XML geometry reads these survey tables ONLY when the bfc "misalign"
option is on. Its entry in BigFullChain.h is

    {"misalign", "", "", "", "", "-AgMLideal", "Misaligned Geometry", kFALSE},

i.e. it works by taking AgMLideal OUT of the chain, which is what lets
AgPosition apply the <Misalign> entries in the geometry XML. Without it the
tables are ignored completely and the geometry is built ideal, however
carefully you edited them -- no warning, no log line, the numbers simply do not
matter.

With "misalign" on, these tables feed BOTH of:

  * fGeom.root generation. build_geom_misalign.C passes misalign=1, so the
    cached geometry is built FROM these tables. The cache is a frozen snapshot:
    once built it no longer consults the tables at all.

  * MC / simulation. The simulated detector is placed with these same
    parameters, so changing a table changes where simulated hits land, not just
    how real hits are reconstructed.

COPYING IS AS DANGEROUS AS LINKING. Both are, for different reasons, and the
copy is the one that actually catches people out:

  * a LINK means someone else's edit, or your own later edit, silently changes
    every job that resolves through it -- reconstruction, geometry building and
    MC alike -- including jobs you meant as a fixed reference.

  * a COPY gives no hint it exists. A symlink at least announces itself in
    "ls -l"; a copied directory looks like an ordinary directory and nothing
    about it says "this is shadowing the release and the database". It also
    goes stale the moment macro/ or the DB is updated, and it keeps winning
    anyway. Weeks later the numbers disagree with everyone else's and the
    reason is a directory you forgot you made.

The real hazard is not copy-versus-link, it is that ./StarDb/Geometry/* EXISTS
AT ALL and silently takes precedence. There is no log line to catch it:
St_db_Maker does not report which of the three sources a table came from, and
StFttDb only says whether it used survey tables, not where they came from
(StFstDb does not even say that much). So check by hand before you trust a
result:

      ls -ld ./StarDb/Geometry/* 2>/dev/null

which prints nothing if you are clean, "d..." if you copied, and the "-> ..."
arrow if you linked. Run it in the job's working directory too, not only where
you submitted from.

Habits that work: keep one working directory per alignment study rather than
editing in place, and delete or rename ./StarDb the moment the study is over,
instead of leaving it for next time.

And be equally careful about WHICH fGeom.root you are using. The cache and the
tables are two separate things that can disagree, and nothing checks that they
match. A stale fGeom built from older tables quietly keeps using the old
alignment while ./StarDb shows the new numbers -- which looks exactly like "the
new alignment had no effect". If you change a table, rebuild the cache, and
name it so the versions are distinguishable: that is what the sdt and label
arguments of build_geom_misalign.C are for.

Pushing to the database
-----------------------
      root4star -b -q 'fstGeometry_db.C("ideal","writedb")'
      root4star -b -q 'fstGeometry_db.C("iter1","writedb")'

Without "writedb" the macro only reads the files and prints what it WOULD
store, which is the safe thing to run first. See the header of
fstGeometry_db.C for the tag -> beginTime mapping and for Gene's rule on
choosing beginTime.

IMPORTANT, read before pushing: the beginTime a table is stored under decides
which runs pick it up, and it is NOT taken from the filename -- the macro sets
it explicitly. The filenames here still use the 2021-11-1x dates we have been
using locally, which do not sit in the slots Gene reserved for ideal and
survey entries. The macro maps them to Gene-compliant times; the filenames
have deliberately NOT been renamed yet, so that existing local setups and
fGeom caches keep working. Settle the rename before the push, not after.
